Review complete. Findings written to `docs/reviews/physics-constants/phys-const-10-btu-per-hr-per-watt.md`.

**Summary**: The HARES constant `3.412_141_633` is the commonly cited NIST rounded value, not the "NIST exact conversion" the comment claims. The full f64 representation should be `3.4121416331279417...` (relative error: `3.75×10⁻¹¹`, ~168,867 ULPs off). EnergyPlus uses the same rounded value, so HARES is consistent with that vendor. Practical impact on simulation is zero; the finding is about labeling accuracy. The fix is straightforward: either write the full-precision literal or derive it from the NIST BTU value already present in the same file (`3600.0 / 1055.055_852_62`).
